# Prototype

Create by **cloning** a prototype. Customize the copy; leave the original alone.

## Anatomy

- `clone()` on the prototype (deep-copy nested data).
- Optional registry (singleton Map of named templates).
- Client: get template → clone → mutate clone.

```ts
export interface Prototype<T> {
  clone(): T;
}

export class ProductTemplate implements Prototype<ProductTemplate> {
  constructor(
    public name: string,
    public price: number,
    public attributes: Record<string, unknown>,
  ) {}

  clone(): ProductTemplate {
    return new ProductTemplate(
      this.name,
      this.price,
      structuredClone(this.attributes),
    );
  }
}
```

Registry as a singleton provider:

```ts
@Injectable()
export class TemplateRegistryService {
  private readonly templates = new Map<string, ProductTemplate>();

  getTemplate(id: string) {
    return this.templates.get(id);
  }

  createFrom(id: string) {
    return this.getTemplate(id)?.clone();
  }
}
```

Use `structuredClone()` (or an explicit field copy). Do not share nested object references between prototype and clone.

## Nest mapping

The registry is a provider. Templates and clones are **plain objects**, not providers.

This is rare in `packages/nest` today: spaces/files are persisted rows, not in-memory templates. Reach for Prototype when seeding many similar in-memory configs or when copy-then-tweak is cheaper than rebuild.

## When to use

- Many variants share most fields.
- Construction is expensive; clone is cheap.
- Templates can be added at runtime.

## When not to use

- Circular graphs / non-cloneable handles (sockets, class instances with identity).
- Each object is unique; a constructor or insert use-case is clearer.

